** Cross zone load balancing 
>> By default ELB distributes load among differnt  AZ irrespective of the no. of register instances present in the AZ. to counter this scenario cross zone load balance came in the picture.
>> Cross Zone load balancing disabled by default. 
>> when Cross zone load balancing is enabled, then ELB will disturbute traffic evenly among register EC2 instances. 
>> ELB  name must be unique in same account. 
>> ELB is region specific , so all registered EC2 instances must be in same region but can be in different AZ. 
>> To define ELB in AZ, you can select one subnet in AZ, subnet can be public and private. 
>> Only one subnet can be defined for ELB in an AZ, if you defined another subnet then previous subnet will be detach from the ELB. 
>> If correct subnet is not connnected to ELB then ELB will not route the traffic to that AZ. 
>> ELB should be accessed by DNS not via IP.
>> ELB can be internet facing or internal ELB.
     ----> Internet facing -- ELB node will have publilc IP.
	 ----> DNS will resolve ELB DNS name to their IP address.
	 ----> It routes traffic to the private IP address of your registered EC2 instances.
	 ----> Intenet facing ELB must have public subnet  so that ELB can route the internet traffic. 
	 ----> Format of the public ELB DNS name of internet facing ELB.
	        name=1234567890.region.elb.amaozonaws.com
	 ----> Format for internal ELB
	       internal=name 123456789.region.elb.amaozonaws.com
		   
		  Note : if nothing is mention before that then it is internet facing ELB











































































































































































 












































































































































































